#edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508000 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "89a28f00-1479-5316-83de-18eb1849ee16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508017 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508017 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508017 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508017 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508017 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507862 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508017 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508017 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508017 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186507704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508017 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bcf0fc0c-4862-5e2c-8784-7233b51e1a00" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508020 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507664 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508020 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508020 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508020 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508020 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508020 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508020 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508020 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186507939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508020 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cb1a33df-5d89-5bd8-acbb-0f22b0025fba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508076 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508000 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508076 #edb/ref 1069 "q-164271" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508076 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508076 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the elliptic paraboloid $z-3=\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{2}.$ Which of the following gives its axis of symmetry, written as an intersection of two planes?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508076 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7fbc634f-eb6c-5cc5-8193-44a06f615641" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508076 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write our equation as \n\n$$\nz-3=\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(y-(-5))^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $z$-axis that has its vertex at $(2,-5,3).$\n Since it is oriented along the $z$-axis, we conclude that its axis of symmetry is parallel to the $z$-axis.\n\nThe axis of symmetry should pass through the vertex of the paraboloid. Therefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry can be written as an intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ y=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508102 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508102 #edb/ref 1069 "q-72190" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508102 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508102 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the vertex of the elliptic paraboloid $z+4=2(x+2)^{2}+5(y-3)^{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508102 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d512c5fc-8dfe-51a8-bc6a-9cb26a626d2d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508102 #edb/ref 1074 "The general equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $z$-axis with vertex at a point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ is \n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo find the vertex, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+4 & =2(x+2)^{2}+5(y-3)^{2} \\\\ z-(-4) & =\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{1/2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{1/5} \\\\ z-(-4) & =\\frac{(x-(-2))^{2}}{1/2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{1/5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic paraboloid that has its vertex at $(-2,3,-4).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508110 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508020 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508110 #edb/ref 1069 "q-164266" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508110 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508110 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the elliptic paraboloid $z+4=\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}$, find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508110 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e5e67c0f-856d-5d0f-b071-fa5f8bea8978" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508110 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the $x$-intercepts of the paraboloid, we substitute $y=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation of the paraboloid and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+4 & =\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{4} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{4}{4} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{3}+1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{3} & =3 \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =9 \\\\ x-4 & =\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solutions $x=1,7.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are $(1,0,0)$ and $(7,0,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508116 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508116 #edb/ref 1069 "q-164280" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508116 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508116 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the trace in the $yz$-plane of the hyperbolic paraboloid $z-1=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508116 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f9f121db-5810-54db-9c64-4a2b5a9ca0c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508116 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the trace in the $yz$-plane, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the paraboloid, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-1 & =\\frac{{0}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2} \\\\ z-1 & =0-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2} \\\\ z-1 & =-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2} \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =-2(z-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives us the equation of the parabola in $yz$-plane." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
